# Subject for tools/check_light_fog.sh: one wall torch, then a cloud of fog
# rolled across the cells between that torch and the cell the check measures.
#
# It dumps TWICE from ONE session, before the fog and after it, so the check
# compares two blocks that share a map, a player position and a source list.
# The two-run A/B that tools/check_light_filter.sh uses for ice cannot do that:
# two runs drift apart as the monsters take their turns.
#
# WHAT IT BUILDS, on seed 1, where chargen-mage.keys lands the mage at (82,13):
#   (79,13)   a wall torch, the only source that reaches the measured cell
#   (80,13)   the crossed cell, clear at the first dump and fog at the second
#   (81,13)   the measured cell, two steps from the torch
#
# The Stinking Cloud has a radius of three, so it also covers the torch and the
# measured cell. That is harmless: the filter walk excludes both endpoints of
# the ray, so only the cells BETWEEN them can take light away.
#
# A mage, not the usual barbarian, because only a caster reaches an FI_FOG
# field. The terrain called "fog" is a different thing (TF_OBSCURE, no field)
# and the light map does not read it.
#
# THREE THINGS THAT BIT tools/keys/lightfilter-show.keys, and bite this too:
#   1. chargen-mage.keys ends on the Entry Chamber room description, so the
#      first key after arrival is spent dismissing it.
#   2. 'w' OPENS the wizard menu; it is not a prefix. The first placement
#      therefore presses K on its own.
#   3. The targeting cursor opens on the player, so one LEFT puts the cloud's
#      centre on (81,13).
#
# Terrain letters from the wizard "Which terrain type?" list: [p] Wall Torch.

@include chargen-mage.keys
@dump:arrival

SPACE
w y

K LEFT*3 ENTER p
@dump:torch-placed

w r "Stinking Cloud" ENTER
@dump:spell-learned

m ENTER
LEFT ENTER
@dump:fog-cast

@quit
